{# What the fare allows, and what the airline allows. The whole article is that
   line: the six rules on a fare come from `fare_brands` and are shown three
   times before you fly, while weights and sizes are the carrier's and are not
   held here at all. See Api\Flights\FareRules. #}
<h2>Every fare includes a personal item</h2>
<p>
    One bag that fits under the seat in front of you, on every fare we sell.
    Nothing else about baggage is fixed. A carry-on bag and a checked bag both
    depend on the fare you pick.
</p>

<h2>Check the fare, not just the price</h2>
<p>
    Each result lists what its fare allows under <strong>What&rsquo;s
    included</strong>, before you fill anything in. Six lines: personal item,
    carry-on bag, checked bag, changes, cancellation and seat choice. Each one
    is marked included, for a fee, or not included.
</p>
<p>
    The same six appear again at checkout, and once more on the booking
    afterwards, so you can always check what you bought.
</p>

<h2>On a connection, the tightest flight decides</h2>
<p>
    A journey allows only what its strictest leg allows. If the second flight
    includes no checked bag, the journey includes no checked bag &mdash; even
    where the first flight would have carried one.
</p>
<p>
    That is why the list at checkout is one list per direction rather than one
    per flight.
</p>

<h2>Weights, sizes and extra bags</h2>
<p>
    Those belong to the airline, and they change by route and by cabin. We do
    not hold them, so check before you pack. Every airline we sell has a page
    here with its phone number on it &mdash; see <a href="/airlines">all
    airlines</a>.
</p>
<p>Extra bags are bought from the airline, not from us.</p>
